@startuml fig_optimistic_vs_pessimistic
!include _style.puml
' Book figure — optimistic version-checking vs pessimistic lock-before-update.
' Form: sequence. Evidence status: conceptual illustration; it draws v2-12's mechanism and
' asserts no rate. The point is that the invariant recheck, not the lock, rejects an
' operation that has become invalid.
title the invariant recheck is what rejects an invalidated operation

participant "reader/writer R1" as R1
participant "reader/writer R2" as R2
database "row (version = 5)" as DB

group optimistic — check the version, retry on a mismatch
  R1 -> DB : 1 read → version 5
  R2 -> DB : 2 read → version 5
  R1 -> DB : 3 UPDATE … WHERE version = 5
  DB --> R1 : 4 one row updated · version → 6
  R2 -> DB : 5 UPDATE … WHERE version = 5
  DB --> R2 : 6 zero rows — rejected
  R2 -> DB : 7 re-read, retry
end

group pessimistic — lock before update
  R1 -> DB : 1 SELECT … FOR UPDATE
  DB --> R1 : 2 lock acquired
  R2 -> DB : 3 SELECT … FOR UPDATE
  note over R2, DB : waits for the lock
  R1 -> R1 : 4 recheck the invariant
  R1 -> DB : 5 update · commit
  DB --> R2 : 6 lock acquired
  R2 -> R2 : 7 recheck the invariant → decide
end

note over R1, DB
  A lock serialises the writers; it does not prove the business condition.
  In both panels it is the invariant recheck that rejects the operation which
  has become invalid.
end note
@enduml
